# Testing Requirements

## ⚠️ MANDATORY TESTING POLICY

**ALL CODE CHANGES MUST INCLUDE TESTS**

- Every new feature must have accompanying tests
- Every bug fix must include a regression test
- **No task is considered complete until all tests pass**
- Pull requests without tests will be rejected

## Testing Strategy

### Test Pyramid
1. **Unit Tests** (70%): Fast, isolated, test individual components
2. **Integration Tests** (20%): Test component interactions
3. **UI Tests** (10%): Test user flows and UI

## Unit Testing Requirements

### What to Test
✅ **Must Have Unit Tests**:
- ViewModels (all state transitions and business logic)
- Use Cases (all business logic paths)
- Repositories (data flow and error handling)
- Utility functions (all edge cases)
- Validation functions (all validation rules)

### Unit Test Structure
```kotlin
class MyViewModelTest {
    // 1. Setup
    private lateinit var viewModel: MyViewModel
    private lateinit var mockUseCase: MockUseCase
    
    @Before
    fun setup() {
        // Initialize mocks and subject under test
    }
    
    // 2. Test cases
    @Test
    fun `test name in backticks describing scenario`() {
        // Given (arrange)
        // When (act)
        // Then (assert)
    }
    
    @After
    fun tearDown() {
        // Clean up if needed
    }
}
```

### Unit Test Naming
- Use descriptive names with backticks
- Format: `` `method name should behavior when condition` ``
- Examples:
  - `` `login should emit success when credentials are valid` ``
  - `` `getUserById should return error when user not found` ``

### Required Test Coverage
- **ViewModels**: ≥ 90% coverage
  - All state changes
  - All user actions
  - All error scenarios
  - Loading states
  
- **Use Cases**: 100% coverage
  - Happy path
  - All error paths
  - Edge cases
  - Validation failures

- **Repositories**: ≥ 85% coverage
  - Data flow (local ↔ remote)
  - Error propagation
  - Caching logic

- **Utilities**: 100% coverage
  - All functions
  - All edge cases
  - Boundary conditions

### Unit Test Tools
- **Mocking**: MockK
- **Coroutines**: `kotlinx-coroutines-test`
- **Flow Testing**: Turbine
- **LiveData/StateFlow**: `androidx.arch.core:core-testing`

### Example Unit Test
```kotlin
class LoginViewModelTest {
    @get:Rule
    val testDispatcherRule = TestDispatcherRule()
    
    @get:Rule
    val instantTaskExecutorRule = InstantTaskExecutorRule()
    
    private lateinit var viewModel: LoginViewModel
    private val loginUseCase = mockk<LoginUseCase>()
    
    @Before
    fun setup() {
        viewModel = LoginViewModel(loginUseCase)
    }
    
    @Test
    fun `login should emit success state when credentials are valid`() = runTest {
        // Given
        val email = "test@example.com"
        val password = "password123"
        coEvery { loginUseCase(email, password) } returns flowOf(
            NetworkResult.Success(User(id = 1, email = email))
        )
        
        // When
        viewModel.onEmailChange(email)
        viewModel.onPasswordChange(password)
        viewModel.onLoginClick()
        
        // Then
        viewModel.uiState.test {
            val state = awaitItem()
            assertTrue(state.isLoginSuccessful)
            assertFalse(state.isLoading)
            assertNull(state.error)
        }
    }
}
```

## UI Testing Requirements

### What to Test
✅ **Must Have UI Tests**:
- Screen composition and layout
- User interactions (clicks, text input)
- Navigation flows
- Error state rendering
- Loading state rendering
- Custom components behavior

### UI Test Structure
```kotlin
@HiltAndroidTest
class LoginScreenTest {
    @get:Rule(order = 0)
    val hiltRule = HiltAndroidRule(this)
    
    @get:Rule(order = 1)
    val composeTestRule = createAndroidComposeRule<MainActivity>()
    
    @Before
    fun setup() {
        hiltRule.inject()
    }
    
    @Test
    fun loginScreen_displaysAllUIElements() {
        // Test UI rendering
    }
    
    @Test
    fun loginScreen_showsErrorWhenInvalidEmail() {
        // Test validation
    }
}
```

### UI Test Tools
- **Compose Test**: `androidx.compose.ui:ui-test-junit4`
- **Espresso**: For non-Compose views
- **Hilt Testing**: `com.google.dagger:hilt-android-testing`
- **MockK**: For mocking dependencies

### UI Test Best Practices
1. Test user-visible behavior, not implementation
2. Use semantic matchers (text, content description)
3. Test accessibility
4. Test different screen sizes (responsive design)
5. Test error and loading states

### Example UI Test
```kotlin
@Test
fun loginScreen_performsLogin_whenValidCredentials() {
    composeTestRule.apply {
        // Enter email
        onNodeWithText("Email").performTextInput("test@example.com")
        
        // Enter password
        onNodeWithText("Password").performTextInput("password123")
        
        // Click login button
        onNodeWithText("Login").performClick()
        
        // Verify navigation
        waitUntil(timeoutMillis = 3000) {
            onAllNodesWithText("User List").fetchSemanticsNodes().isNotEmpty()
        }
    }
}
```

## Test Execution Requirements

### Before Committing
```bash
# Run all unit tests
./gradlew test

# Run all instrumented tests
./gradlew connectedAndroidTest

# Generate test coverage report
./gradlew jacocoTestReport
```

### Continuous Integration
- All tests must pass in CI pipeline
- Minimum code coverage thresholds must be met
- No warnings or errors in test execution

## Test Documentation

### Test Files Location
- **Unit Tests**: `app/src/test/java/`
- **UI Tests**: `app/src/androidTest/java/`
- **Test Utilities**: `app/src/test/java/.../TestData.kt`, `TestDispatcher.kt`

